00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
or in the National Eagle Scouts Association's

121
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Eagle Letter-

122
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
And we're going to talk about another book

123
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
that Mark wrote about.

124
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
that you probably don't know about,

125
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
but you should in just a moment.

126
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Hey, Mark, how you doing?

127
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
I'm doing great. How about you, Clark?

128
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Oh, we're doing all right.

129
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
You're down there in Louisville, Kentucky,

130
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Is that right?

131
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
That's correct.

132
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
So down there in Louisville

133
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
it must be about as hot as it is here

134
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
in Kennett Square this morning.

135
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Yeah, it's definitely summer camp season.

136
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
It's summer camp weather.

137
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Let's say that. I'll give you that.

138
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
So tell me a little bit about

139
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
your background in Scouting, Mark.

140
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Okay,

141
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
I joined Scouting in the third grade-

142
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Back when Scouting still started

143
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
in the third grade-

144
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And went all through the program

145
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,000
in Oxford and Mississippi

146
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
and became an Eagle Scout.

147
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
I worked with my home troop through college

148
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
and then, after I graduated

149
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
from my bachelor's program,

150
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
my master's program decided that

151
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
I would give professional Scouting a try,

152
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And so I was a district executive

153
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
for about five years, both in Fort Myers, Florida,

154
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
and here in Louisville,

155
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And enjoyed that a lot.

156
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
but as I went along

157
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
I realized that the things that I was really good at

158
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and enjoyed were sort of the side projects

159
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
more than the fundraising and the recruiting

160
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
that are key to the district effectiveness job,

161
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And so I jumped out of that

162
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
and back into the volunteer ranks

163
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
And then an adult volunteer

164
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
for about 25 years I guess,

165
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
mostly with Boy Scouting.

166
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
I was a scout master for five years

167
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And I did a lot of things there

168
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
and currently I'm on our troop committee,

169
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
also on our PAC committee

170
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
and chair our Venturing Crew committee.

171
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So I'm involved in all three sites of the program right now.

172
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
That's a lot of hat,

173
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And a few others as well.

174
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Not all that uncommon out there

175
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
in the world of Scouting

176
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
where a couple of different hats.

177
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
So somewhere along the way

178
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
you decided that we needed

179
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
another Scout Master's Handbook.

180
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,000
What about?

181
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I guess a couple of things.

182
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
First of all,

183
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,000
especially when I was a district executive,

184
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
I would look around at a lot of Scout Masters

185
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and realize that a lot of them were struggling,

186
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Some were disorganized,

187
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
some were going it alone

188
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
and really didn't have the tools

189
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
to recruit and empower volunteers.

190
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
A lot of folks get really focused

191
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
on one or two of the methods of Scouting

192
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
and the exclusion of the others,

193
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
So they end up with a really unbalanced program.

194
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
Then, when I looked at the Scout Master Handbook-

195
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
the last several editions-

196
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
I realized that it's intended

197
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
primarily for new Scout Masters,

198
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
which is an intentional decision

199
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
that National has made

200
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and makes sense, because those are the folks

201
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
that go to the Scout Shop

202
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and are looking for resources

203
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and they need to know the basics:

204
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,000
What is a patrol,

205
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
What is a troop,

206
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
What is a board review and so forth-

207
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
But there's not a lot in there

208
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,000
that talks about

209
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
so what do you do when things go wrong?

210
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
And that's the sort of information

211
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,000
that I wanted to give.

212
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,000
And also, over the years

213
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
I just picked up a lot of ideas

214
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
that I thought were valuable,

215
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
that I wanted to be able to share with folks.

216
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
What's some of the common feedback you get

217
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
or common questions you get about the book?

218
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,000
A lot of what I get

219
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
is from experienced leaders who say

220
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
I didn't think I'd find anything useful in here,

221
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,000
but there were really some things

222
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
that I found useful.

223
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
I think a common reaction is:

224
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
wow. this lets me know that I'm not alone.

225
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Everybody runs into these same issues

226
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
that I've been dealing with,

227
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Because you know,

228
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
one of the things that's interesting

229
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
about working in a Scout unit

230
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
is you often really have tunnel vision

231
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
and you just know what's happening in your unit.

232
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
You go around the table,

233
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
you see people at camp freeze and so forth,

234
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
But you rarely go to another troop's meeting

235
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
and see it in action.

236
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
But you don't realize

237
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
that everybody's dealing with common struggles.

238
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
It takes a little bit of an ego

239
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
to be a unit leader.

240
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
whether you're a Cub master or a Scout master or whatever.

241
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
We're not real likely to go looking for help

242
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
a lot of times.

243
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Is there a particular part of the book

244
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
that you've gotten a lot of comments on

245
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
or that has been really

246
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
relevant for folks?

247
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
One of the sections of the book,

248
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and also something that

249
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
one I've shot a film on,

250
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
has really seemed to be important to folks

251
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:13,000
is this idea of achieving some balance

252
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
in your program.

253
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
I guess it's a two-star process.

254
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,000
First of all

255
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
is remembering the distinction

256
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
between the aims and the methods.

257
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
The aims are citizenship, character

258
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and fitness.

259
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
The methods are camping

260
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and uniforming and advancement

261
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,000
and adult association

262
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and all those sorts of things.

263
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
And some folks get those mixed up

264
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and are so focused on

265
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
teaching kids how to camp

266
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
that they forget about developing character.

267
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
You've got these eight methods

268
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
and you've got to come up

269
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,000
with some sort of balance.

270
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
You can have an extreme where

271
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
you're so focused on developing youth leaders

272
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
that you're totally delegating

273
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
leadership to the PLC,

274
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
which is great,

275
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
but if they drop the ball

276
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and you don't go camping all year,

277
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
things are really out of whack.

278
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
Or the other extreme:

279
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
is the Scott Masters really hands-on,

280
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
has a really really great

281
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,000
outdoor program,

282
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
but his junior leaders are figureheads?

283
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Do we spend enough time on that

284
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
in leadership training? do you think?

285
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
I don't think we do.

286
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I've been on a task force

287
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,000
at the national level

288
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
that specifically looking at how

289
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
we need the character development aim.

290
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
We don't spend a lot of time

291
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,000
talking about character.

292
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
We spend a lot of time talking

293
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
about camping techniques

294
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
and the administration

295
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and so forth.

296
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
We don't really talk a lot

297
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
about intentionally teaching character.

298
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
One of the things

299
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
that we have done at Fillmont

300
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
is to hand out a worksheet

301
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
that has each of the values

302
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,000
from the oath and the law.

303
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,000
So we say, okay,

304
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
come up with a list of ways

305
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
that you can intentionally teach

306
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
the value of thrift

307
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
or the value of hearing loss.

308
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,000
And guys will say,

309
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
well, you could do a Scott Master Minute.

310
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
But when you get beyond that,

311
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:05,000
it really gets challenging.

312
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
When you start brainstorming,

313
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,000
you think, well,

314
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
there's some really neat stuff we could do.

315
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
talking about obedient:

316
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Well, if we're going to be obedient

317
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:14,000
and we're going to set an example,

318
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
we probably should take our radar detectors

319
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
out of our cars

320
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
when we go on camping trips.

321
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Yeah, there you go.

322
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
That hit though

323
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Exactly.

324
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
And then somebody else said,

325
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
well, with trustworthy,

326
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
how about we don't have any padlocks

327
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
on our footlockers at summer camps?

328
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
There are some things

329
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
that we could intentionally do

330
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:38,000
that would model and teach these values

331
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
more than just giving a Scott Master Minute

332
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
at the end of the meeting

333
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
or repeating the oath and the law

334
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
at the opening.

335
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And the other thing is you say,

336
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
well, they get the values naturally

337
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:51,000
in the program

338
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And that's true,

339
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
But that doesn't mean that we can't push it along

340
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,000
a little bit more.

341
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Tell me about your philosophy

342
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
of working with youth leaders

343
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and what you think are the most important

344
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
two or three bullet points

345
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
for people to understand about that.

346
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
I think the first, most important point

347
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
is to have a really good understanding

348
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
of where your youth leaders are

349
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and come up alongside them

350
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
and provide them the support that they need.

351
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
I had one senior patrol leader years ago

352
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
who was really a natural born leader-

353
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,000
such a thing-

354
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and we would spend 20 minutes on the phone

355
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
talking about what was going to happen

356
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Thursday night.

357
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
In the course of that conversation

358
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
he was prepared for that meeting

359
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:35,000
and I didn't really have to worry.

360
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
We've had other senior patrol leaders.

361
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
He almost had to stand beside and whisper:

362
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
okay, now it's time to do this,

363
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
Now it's time to do that,

364
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And you've got to figure out

365
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
which approach works for that one scout

366
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
rather than just having a one-size-fits-all approach.

367
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
One of the more interesting statements

368
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
is: you point out that youth leadership

369
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
is a method and not an aim.

370
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,000
What does that mean?

371
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,000
It's interesting.

372
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
I think if you interviewed a lot of scout masters,

373
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
they would say that creating leaders

374
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
is what we're all about,

375
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
and it's really not

376
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
because a lot of our kids are not going to be

377
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
quote-unquote leaders in their life.

378
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
They're going to be followers

379
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
and we want them to be good followers

380
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
just as much as we want them to be good leaders.

381
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
And when we focus so much

382
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
on the youth leadership method,

383
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
I think that's where the other pieces

384
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
of the program can fall to the side.

385
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
One of the hardest achievements

386
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
for an adult leader

387
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
is being able to first see that that's important

388
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
and then actually be able to make it happen.

389
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Let me add just one other thing

390
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
on working with youth leaders

391
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
that I think is really important.

392
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Sometimes, when there's a work in the action,

393
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
when it's time for the closing

394
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:50,000
and nothing's happening,

395
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
our first reaction is to jump in there

396
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and fix the situation.

397
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
But unless somebody's bleeding,

398
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
sometimes we just need to let things happen,

399
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
And that's where leadership is formed.

400
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
when those kids have to deal with those situations,

401
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
We need to give them the same opportunity

402
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
to figure that stuff out as we've had,

403
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and sometimes that means not fixing it.

404
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
If you're sort of trigger happy,

405
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
maybe you've got to count to 10

406
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
or count to 50 before you do anything.

407
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
No, I'm pretty trigger happy, Mark,

408
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,000
I don't know about you.

409
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
I wanted to ask you about

410
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
rethinking the ideal patrol size.

411
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
You talk about the difference

412
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
between what's recommended as a patrol size

413
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
and what really works.

414
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
The program tells you that eight scouts

415
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,000
is the ideal patrol.

416
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
If you go to Wood Badge,

417
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,000
if you go to NYLT,

418
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
that's the ideal.

419
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
The problem these days

420
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
is that our kids so often are so over-scheduled.

421
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
I don't think in most cases

422
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
kids are making as many troop meetings

423
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
and outings as they did

424
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
when you and I were in scouts.

425
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Suddenly, your eight scout patrol

426
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
gets reduced to three scouts

427
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
or four scouts or two scouts

428
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,000
on a camp out.

429
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
What people seem to usually do

430
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
is say: well, we'll combine

431
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
the shark patrol and the flaming air patrol

432
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:14,000
for this one camp out

433
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and have a provisional patrol

434
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:17,000
which gets the job done,

435
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
but it destroys the patrol identity.

436
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
My argument is: one way to address that problem

437
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
is, instead of having eight guys in the patrol,

438
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
have 12 guys in the patrol, let's say,

439
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
with the idea that,

440
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
okay, if half of them don't show up on the camp out,

441
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
then you've got six

442
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and that's enough to get the job done

443
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
without having to mess up the patrol's identity.

444
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,000
I think that's really wise.

445
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
I think things have changed significantly,

446
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
that we really need to look at patrol structure

447
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,000
in a different way.

448
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The other book that we mentioned in the introduction

449
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
was the Eagle Court of Honor book.

450
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
How did that book come about?

451
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,000
That again goes back to my district executive days.

452
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
One of the things you do as a DE

453
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
is go to a lot of courts of honor,

454
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
and I went to some that were really good

455
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and some that were pretty horrific.

456
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Two cases in particular

457
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
that sort of crystallized the need for me

458
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
One. I went to a court of honor

459
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,000
where there were two scouts being recognized.

460
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
Somebody from the troop committee got up

461
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
at the end of the ceremony

462
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and read all the congratulatory letters

463
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
that one of the scouts had received.

464
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Then he read the other set,

465
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
which of course were identical word for word

466
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
to the first set.

467
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Oh man,

468
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,000
I've read them all.

469
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
I have never experienced that before.

470
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Kind of difficult courts of honor before,

471
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
but that's a new winner right there.

472
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:39,000
And then there was another one,

473
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and this was actually in my district.

474
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:42,000
There was a set of twins

475
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
who were both receiving their badges

476
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And for some reason in this ceremony

477
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
they had the guys go up on stage

478
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,000
at the very beginning and stand at attention.

479
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Oh no,

480
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Oh, I know how this goes.

481
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Okay, go ahead.

482
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
I'm sorry.

483
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
At some point in the ceremony

484
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
the first one passed out.

485
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Oh, no,

486
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Almost, don't cue. the other one passed out,

487
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
Oh man,

488
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
Oh,

489
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Yeah,

490
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:09,000
Yeah.

491
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
Well, that would be two good reasons

492
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,000
to write a book right there.

493
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
A third reason came up after I wrote it,

494
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and I had no idea about this,

495
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
but there are many, many troops out there

496
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
that basically delegate the whole process

497
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,000
to the family.

498
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Yeah,

499
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,000
How about that? huh,

500
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Yeah.

501
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I've heard from

502
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
I don't know how many parents who said:

503
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
I've never even been to an Eagle Court of Honor,

504
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Mm-hmm,

505
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
And they want me to plan this thing.

506
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,000
Help,

507
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:38,000
Yeah,

508
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:39,000
What,

509
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,000
What of what?

510
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:44,000
The book is a collection of very practical advice

511
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and then some scripts and things like that as well.

512
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Mm-hmm,

513
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
What's your best advice for somebody

514
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,000
who's looking at scheduling

515
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
and presenting in an Eagle Court of Honor?

516
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,000
To go on my website.

517
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
I've got what's called

518
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
the Eagle Court of Honor Backdater.

519
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,000
It's a free interactive tool

520
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
where you plug in the date

521
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
that you're going to hold the Court of Honor

522
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and it tells you basically everything that you need to do,

523
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
from the board review date

524
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
to sending thank you notes,

525
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
But it also helps put things in perspective,

526
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Like you need to reserve the place

527
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
You're going to hold the ceremony first

528
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
before you submit an invitation.

529
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
You don't need to worry

530
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
about getting the programs printed

531
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,000
until much later in the process, for example

532
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:29,000
Mm-hmm.

533
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
So it gives you kind of a to-do list

534
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
and helps you make it a little bit simpler.

535
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Exactly, yeah,

536
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Okay,

537
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
Because I'll make sure to have a link to that.

538
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
What kind of feedback do you get

539
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
about the Eagle Court of Honor book?

540
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:42,000
People are thrilled.

541
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:43,000
What I like about it-

542
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and I hope this is what people like about it as well-

543
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,000
is I sort of talk through the parts of the ceremony

544
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
and then I give people a bunch of mix and match

545
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
segments that they could plug in

546
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
to create a ceremony

547
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
unique for their recipient.

548
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
I think every Eagle Court of Honor

549
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:01,000
needs to be different

550
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
because every recipient is different.

551
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Let's see if we can kill

552
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
a couple of urban legends

553
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
of scouting here real quick.

554
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
There's no official format that you have to follow

555
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
or anything like that,

556
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
And you don't even have to have a court of honor.

557
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
You are an Eagle Scout

558
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
once you pass the board review.

559
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
That's the second Urban Legend

560
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
is there's no kind of incantation

561
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
or ceremonial something or other

562
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
that happens at a court of honor

563
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,000
that makes somebody into an Eagle Scout.

564
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:37,000
That's probably when you look at the certificate

565
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:38,000
that comes from National.

566
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,000
the date that appears there is the board of review date,

567
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:44,000
because that's when you become an Eagle Scout.

568
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
The third book is maybe not quite so well known

569
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:47,000
as the first two.

570
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
It's called

571
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Growing Up Right, Growing Up Strong-

572
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
The Parents' Guide for Scouting.

573
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,000
How did that come about?

574
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:57,000
It's part of the Scout Parents' Initiative,

575
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
which was actually started

576
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
by a longtime scouter named Gerald Longhorn

577
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
passed away a couple of years ago.

578
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
He had this vision that

579
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
parents needed to be more a part

580
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
of the scouting program

581
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
And basically what we wanted to do in the book

582
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
is help people understand

583
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
how scouting supports the family.

584
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,000
So I interviewed parents of Scouts

585
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,000
literally from around the world,

586
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
because we tracked some folks down

587
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
who have been involved in scouting

588
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
as expatriates in the military

589
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:31,000
and talked about what scouting does for kids,

590
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,000
what scouting does for families

591
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:37,000
and what scouting does for parents.

592
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
in that sort of a call to action

593
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
of what parents can do for scouting,

594
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
Put together sort of a little decision tree

595
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
that folks who are thinking about getting involved

596
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:50,000
can use to figure out how the best could serve.

597
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:51,000
So it's got categories-

598
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
How much time do you have available?

599
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
Do you prefer to work with your son,

600
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
with adults, bookkeeping and finance-

601
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
and then it sort of directs them

602
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:05,000
from those attributes to particular positions

603
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
in the unit where they could serve.

604
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
And this book is free.

605
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
We can download it as a PDF file.

606
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
There'll be a link on the post

607
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
that contains this podcast.

608
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
You made it up to the National Jamboree last year.

609
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:18,000
I did,

610
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
And what did you do there?

611
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:21,000
I was on the staff

612
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
at the National Eagle Scout Association booth.

613
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
What was happening at the booth?

614
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
But the real highlight was that

615
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
just about every day, we had at least

616
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
one nationally prominent Eagle Scout on hand.

617
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,000
Oh, okay,

618
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
And those guys were extremely gracious

619
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:37,000
to sign autographs, to answer questions.

620
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
Some of them would get up on the podium

621
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:43,000
and speak for a few minutes,

622
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
And then the real highlight was

623
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
that we presented the Distinguished Eagle Scout Award

624
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
to Colonel Charles McGee,

625
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
who is one of the original Tuskegee Airmen

626
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
from World War II.

627
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
Planning on going in 2013 up to the summit,

628
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
I think. so

629
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
I'm excited about what's going on there

630
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
and really want to get a look at it firsthand.

631
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
You've been around scouting now

632
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:09,000
for 25, almost 30 years as a leader.

633
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:12,000
What do you think has changed significantly

634
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
about scouting?

635
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,000
I think technology and how we do training,

636
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
how we communicate with our kids-

637
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
that's really changed, potentially for the better.

638
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
I think in terms of the organization,

639
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
the summit is a really good example

640
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
of how, more than ever, we're really looking

641
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
at what kids want and need

642
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:33,000
and tailoring the program to meet their needs

643
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
and also to be maybe more relevant

644
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
and exciting than it was 30 years ago.

645
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
Been really exciting to see that

646
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
and I think that's going to carry through

647
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
across the organization.

648
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
If I gave you the ability to talk to every new scout leader

649
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
and you had 30 seconds to talk to them

650
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
and tell them everything that you know,

651
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:53,000
what would be the most important points you would hit on?

652
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:57,000
You are uniquely positioned to make a real difference

653
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
in some young person's life,

654
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
More than their parents, perhaps

655
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
more than their Sunday school teacher, perhaps

656
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:07,000
certainly more than their soccer coach

657
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
or their piano teacher

658
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
or their teacher in school.

659
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
There are just so many examples of folks

660
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
who look back and say that their life

661
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
was drastically changed

662
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:22,000
because of an adult who took an interest in them

663
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
and saw potential in them.

664
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,000
I guess I would say: stick with it.

665
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
It's a hard job-

666
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
That's why we get paid so much-

667
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
But impact kids' lives

668
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
and you can impact the future of our country

669
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
and train the world as a result.

670
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
That is excellent advice.

671
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
It sets the entire stage

672
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
for everything you're going to do. as a volunteer leader,

673
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
You're uniquely positioned

674
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,000
to be able to actually make a difference.

675
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
I really like that.

676
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
That's a good choice.

677
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
Let me share a little story related to that.

678
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:59,000
One of the folks that I interviewed a while back

679
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
for the Eagle Letter was a guy named Chuck Smith

680
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
and when he was growing up

681
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
in inner city Los Angeles-

682
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,000
in the 50s I guess it was-

683
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,000
he was shy, he was dyslexic,

684
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
he had learning disabilities,

685
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
but he wanted to be somebody

686
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:19,000
and to do something. and he went up to his teacher

687
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:22,000
and shared with her that he wanted to go to college

688
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
and maybe get into radio

689
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:27,000
or the space program or something.

690
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
and she looked at him and said:

691
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
get out of here. you're going to be lucky

692
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
if you can get out of high school.

693
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
He was crushed by that, obviously.

694
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
but his parents had a friend

695
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
who was a ham radio operator

696
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
and was a scout master

697
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,000
and this guy said to Chuck:

698
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
you can not only learn about radio

699
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
but you can learn about life

700
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
and I've got just the organization for you,

701
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
which, of course, was scouting.

702
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
Chuck learned about radio.

703
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
he learned about communications

704
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
a couple of years ago, retired as the president

705
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
of AT&T West,

706
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
and he's been an incredible mentor

707
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
to a lot of inner city young people

708
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
who are in the same situation that he was in,

709
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:11,000
and that all happened because that one scout leader

710
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:14,000
took an interest in him and saw the potential.

711
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:17,000
It is pretty awesome and not in the

712
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
late 1980s sense of the term,

713
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:22,000
but it is like really awesome

714
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:25,000
what can happen and the kind of influence

715
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,000
and the good that a person can do

716
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
as a scouting volunteer.

717
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Well, I really appreciate you spending the time with us.

718
